"What does the example callback code look like for the invoice router, and what does it do?" — needs code block kept together with its surrounding explanation.

"What parameters does the callback path operation decorator take in the example, and why?" — tests whether a chunk boundary separates the code from the explanation of why those parameters are used.

"How does the callback URL get parameterized using the request body?" — spans explanation + example, good test for whether adjacent chunks get retrieved together.

Attention Is All You Need

"What is the formula for scaled dot-product attention, and why is the scaling factor used?" — tests whether the equation and its justification (often in a separate sentence/paragraph) end up in the same chunk.

"How does multi-head attention differ from single-head attention in terms of computation?" — spans a conceptual explanation plus the underlying math, good test of section-boundary handling.

"What positional encoding method did the authors use, and why did they choose it over learned embeddings?" — tests retrieval across what's often two adjacent paragraphs (method description + justification/comparison).

Pride and Prejudice

"Why does Elizabeth initially refuse Darcy's first proposal?" — needs a full scene/exchange kept intact, not split mid-dialogue.

"How does Elizabeth's opinion of Darcy shift after she reads his letter?" — spans two scenes (the proposal, then the letter), testing whether retrieval can pull both relevant chunks even if they're pages apart.

"What role does Mr. Collins' proposal to Elizabeth play in the plot, and how does she respond?" — tests whether a comedic, dialogue-heavy scene stays coherent as a single retrievable unit.

Biglari Holdings 10-K

"What were total revenues and net earnings for the most recent fiscal year?" — tests whether a financial table is retrieved as an intact, correctly-parsed unit rather than fragmented rows.

"What risk factors does the company identify related to its insurance operations?" — tests section-aware chunking (Risk Factors section should be cleanly delineated).

"How does management explain the change in earnings from the prior year?" — tests MD&A section retrieval, which often references numbers from a table elsewhere in the doc